01:13:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question, and it’s actually based on a very common myth! The idea that we taste sweet only at the tip of the tongue and bitter only at the back comes from an old diagram called the **“tongue map.”** You’ve probably seen it in textbooks—it shows sweet on the tip, salty and sour on the sides, and bitter on the back. But modern science has shown that this map is completely wrong. ### How the myth started The tongue map originated from a German paper published in 1901. The researcher, D.P. Hänig, measured sensitivity thresholds for different tastes on various parts of the tongue. He found tiny differences—for example, the tip was *slightly* more sensitive to sweet, and the back was *slightly* more sensitive to bitter. But these differences were very small, and he never said that other areas couldn’t taste those flavors. Later, a psychologist misinterpreted his data and turned it into a simplified drawing that got copied again and again. ### The reality of taste We now know that **all tastes can be detected everywhere on the tongue that has taste buds** (the center of the tongue has none, but the edges, tip, and back do). Every taste bud contains taste receptor cells that can respond to sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami. While some areas might be a tiny bit more sensitive to certain tastes, the difference is so minor that in everyday life it’s unnoticeable. You can prove this yourself: try putting a drop of lemon juice on the tip of your tongue—you’ll taste sourness just fine! So the real answer is: we don’t taste sweet only at the tip and bitter only at the back. The tongue map is a stubborn myth, but taste is actually a full-tongue experience.
